restrictions on genetically modified  organisms are high on the agenda as EU  environment ministers meet in Luxembourg  the council is expected to reach a  political agreement on rules that will  allow member states to restrict or  prohibit the cultivation of some GMOs on  their territories even if they're  authorized at EU level the aims to  provide a legal basis to limit GMO  cultivation on grounds other than health  or environmental considerations  conditions which are already taken into  account in the current rules while  they're widely grown in the Americas and  Asia genetically modified crops are  generally unpopular in Europe  environmentalists have raised concerns  about the impact on biodiversity  Europe's clear air program is also up  for discussion this time in public  debate the program sets limits on  harmful emissions from industry traffic  energy plants and agriculture The  ministers will express their views on a  proposal for new rules on medium-sized  combustion plants which are used for  residential heating and cooling and  providing heat or steam for industry  they'll also look at national air  pollutant emission ceilings
 

